TITLE:: NdefRackModuleView
summary:: one module in the rack window, drawn in a single view
categories:: Libraries>NdefRack
related:: Classes/NdefRackGUI, Classes/NdefRackModule, Classes/NdefRackDisplay

DESCRIPTION::
The window makes one for each module; you do not make them yourself.

A whole module - title, ports, bars, switches, buttons, arrays, a display - is
drawn in one link::Classes/UserView::, one widget however much it holds. It
keeps no copy of any value: every redraw reads its
link::Classes/NdefRackModule::, and every gesture writes through the module to
the Ndef, so whatever changed the Ndef shows on the next frame.

code::
 ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │[>] flt                       lpf          [x]│  play, name, library module, delete
 ○  in  ← osc                    ▪▪  ar 1       ●  main row: main input | output
 │ ──────────────────────────────────────────── │  a gap between bands
 ◇    [freq ██████████│▌·········     1.2 kHz]  │  a bar: name, fill, value
 ◆    [res ← lfo  ┊░░░▐░░░┊··△········   0.41]  │  patched: the live value
 │ ──────────────────────────────────────────── │
 ▫    [trig                                 ▪]  │  a gate or a trigger: a button
 └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
::

Everything is in one column, link::#*colW:: wide - link::#*displayW:: with a
display. Under the title, a bus module or a panner has its drop-down bar
(link::#-busLabel::, link::#-speakersLabel::). Then the strong::main row::: the
module's first audio input, on the left half, and its output, on the right half
(link::#-outputText::), on one line - so a chain placed left to right wires
straight across. A display - scope, spectrum - gets its
link::Classes/NdefRackDisplay:: and its picture straight under the main row.
Then a row per control, in the order link::Classes/NdefRackModule#-ordered::
gives - main inputs, timing, the body, the tail - with a hairline gap before
each band. A module whose controls change gets a new view in the same place.

Every input is a port on the left edge, the output a port on the right edge. A
port's shape says what it carries - a circle for audio, a diamond for control, a
square for a gate or a trigger - hollow while empty, filled in the cable's
colour once plugged, with a second ring when it has more than one channel. A row
is drawn by its param's role (link::Classes/NdefRackModule#*roleFor::), so the
same kind of control looks the same on every module:

table::
## strong::role:: || strong::row::
## code::\signal:: || the port and the name; where its cable comes from
## code::\bar:: || a bar filling from the left - from a zero line when the range goes below 0 - with a notch at the default, the name at the left and the value in its units at the right (link::Classes/NdefRackSpec#*readout::); grey at the default, white once changed
## code::\stepped:: || a bar with a tick per step, up to 16; the whole number, or its name when the library module names its stops
## code::\toggle:: || a bar with a switch reading on or off, or its two names
## code::\array:: || two rows: the name - with code::index: value:: under the mouse - and a bar per value; cells to click when the spec steps by 1
## code::\trigger:: || a button with the control's own name and an arrow; it flashes when it fires
## code::\gate:: || a button with the control's own name and a lamp
::

A control with voices has three short lines before its name, and a thin line
per voice when the voices differ. A controller element turning a control is
named in its bar, just left of the value.

A strong::patched:: row takes no drag. Its bar has no fill but a dashed outline
in the cable's colour, its name reads code::res ← lfo::, a line marks the value
arriving now - one per voice - over a pale band of the range the cable covered
in the last link::Classes/NdefRackGUI#*historyLength:: frames (a control rate
source on a local server), and a small triangle under the bar marks the
module's own value, the one it goes back to when unplugged.

table::
## strong::gesture:: || strong::does::
## click the title or the plain face || selects the module; with shift, adds it to the selection or takes it out
## drag the title or the plain face || moves the selection, on a 10 pixel grid
## the play button || plays or stops an audio rate module
## the x || deletes the module - cmd-z brings it back, like every other step (link::Classes/NdefRackUndo::)
## drag a bar left or right || the value, from where it is: the bar's width is the whole range, in the spec's own steps; with shift ten times finer; nothing under 4 pixels
## click a bar's value || a field over it to type a value - link::#-editField::
## double click a bar or a switch || the function's default
## click a switch || on or off
## click and drag an array || every bar the mouse passes; a cell flips, and a drag paints that state on
## hold a button || a trigger fires; a gate is 1 until let go
## click a bus module's drop-down || BusManager's buses, the one it reads or writes to ticked; strong::a bus index...:: types one in, at the width and rate it has now; for an index, strong::channels:: and strong::rate::
## drag from the output || a cable, dropped on a row or a module's title - link::#-titleInput::
## drag from an input's port || a cable to any other module's output; a plugged input is picked up, to move or unplug
## click the output or a port, then click a target || the same cable, with no button held between - see link::Classes/NdefRackGUI#-armedCable::
## right click || copy, cut, duplicate, delete, as code, unplug, open in NdefGui; on a control: reset, type a value, the set line as code
::

A press in the narrow strip between a port and its bar does nothing. In learn
mode (link::Classes/NdefRackKtl::) a click on a control, gate or trigger -
its port included - arms it for a controller before anything else.

Moving the mouse over the view puts a hint in the window's
link::Classes/NdefRackHintBar:: about whatever is under it - link::#-hintAt::.
Over a module, the module's hint always wins over a cable running behind it.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: colW
The width of a module, 190.

METHOD:: displayW
The width of a module with a scope or spectrum picture, 300.

METHOD:: displayH
How tall a scope or spectrum picture is, 120.

METHOD:: titleH
The height of the title bar, 26.

METHOD:: rowH
The height of a row - the main row and every control's, 24.

METHOD:: arrayH
The height of an array's row, 48: its name, and the bars under it.

METHOD:: busH
The height of a bus module's or a panner's drop-down bar, 24.

METHOD:: gapH
The gap before each band of rows after the first, 6, with a hairline in it.

METHOD:: portR
How big a port is drawn, its radius: 5, and 7 while a cable it could take is loose.

METHOD:: colName
The colour of a control's name.

METHOD:: colQuiet
The colour of a value at its default, and of a controller's tag.

METHOD:: colChanged
The colour of a value once it is not the default.


METHOD:: pageRows
How many control rows past the main inputs a module shows before it has pages,
and how many a page holds. 8.

METHOD:: tabH
How tall the strip of tabs is. 22.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: module
The link::Classes/NdefRackModule::.

METHOD:: gui
The link::Classes/NdefRackGUI:: it is in.

METHOD:: view
The link::Classes/UserView::.

METHOD:: display
The link::Classes/NdefRackDisplay::, for a scope or spectrum module; nil for any other.

METHOD:: editField
The field open over a value being typed in, or nil.

METHOD:: key
The module's key.

METHOD:: origin
Where it sits on the canvas.

METHOD:: bounds
Its rect on the canvas.

METHOD:: pages
Its pages - Events code::(name:, bands:, count:):: - when it has more controls
past its main inputs than link::#*pageRows::; nil when it has one. A module
whose file says code::pages:: (link::Classes/NdefRackLib#*pagesFor::) gets those;
any other has its bands kept whole on the fewest, most evenly filled pages.

METHOD:: page
The page shown, from 0.

METHOD:: pageFor
The page a control is on, or nil when it has no pages.
ARGUMENT:: name
The control.

METHOD:: isCompact
Whether it was laid out compact: its main row and only the controls away from
their defaults (link::Classes/NdefRack#*setCompact::).

METHOD:: isStale
Compact, laid out for other controls than the ones away from their defaults
now, and no gesture under way: the window makes the view again at the next
frame.

METHOD:: hiddenCount
How many controls a compact view leaves out.

METHOD:: tagColor
The colour the module is marked with (link::Classes/NdefRack#*setColor::), as a
link::Classes/Color::, or nil.

METHOD:: size
How big it is, a link::Classes/Point::.

METHOD:: moveTo
Put it somewhere else on the canvas.
ARGUMENT:: point
a link::Classes/Point::, the top left.

METHOD:: refresh
Draw it again.

METHOD:: remove
Take the view away and free its display.

METHOD:: inputJack
The centre of an input's port, in the view's own coordinates: always at x 8, on
the left edge. A cable meets the view at its left edge, level with it.
ARGUMENT:: param
the control's name.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Point::, or nil for a name that is not an input.

METHOD:: outputJack
The centre of the output port: at the right edge, on the main row. A cable leaves
the view at its right edge, level with it.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Point::.

METHOD:: inputAt
The control whose row is under a point - the whole row counts, not only its
port, so a cable dropped on a bar plugs into that control - or nil.
ARGUMENT:: point
a link::Classes/Point:: in the view's own coordinates.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Symbol::, or nil.

METHOD:: isOverOutput
Whether a point is in the output zone, the right half of the main row: a press
there starts a cable, and in the other direction a cable dropped there connects.
ARGUMENT:: point
a link::Classes/Point:: in the view's own coordinates.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Boolean::.

METHOD:: titleInput
Where a cable dropped on this module's title lands: its first main input
(link::Classes/NdefRackModule#-bands::, the first band) that is free and takes
what the cable carries - audio into an audio input, control into the rest -
else its first main input, else nil.
ARGUMENT:: from
the key of the module the cable comes from.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Symbol::, or nil.

METHOD:: dropHint
What letting a cable go on a control would do, in words: code::lfo → oct of
lpf::, then what else there is to know - the cable it replaces, audio into a
control input, a module patched into itself (one block late), voices of which
only the first arrives. The window shows it in the hint bar while the cable is
loose.
ARGUMENT:: src
the key of the module the cable comes from.
ARGUMENT:: param
the control it would land on.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String::.

METHOD:: outputText
What the output says: its rate and width - code::ar 1::, code::kr 4::,
code::through ar 2:: on a scope or spectrum - or its channels' names, joined
by a middle dot, when the library module names them and they fit:
code::value · gate:: on seq8 (link::Classes/NdefRackLib#*outsFor::).
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String::.

METHOD:: cableHint
What the hint bar says over a patched input: where its cable comes from and, with voices, which voice gets which channel.
ARGUMENT:: param
the control's name.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String::.

METHOD:: hintAt
What the hint bar says about a point in the view: the title, a control with its
value, default, range and what a drag or a click does, a port and where its cable
comes from - with, on a patched row, the value it returns to when unplugged -
the output and its channels, the picture.
ARGUMENT:: point
a link::Classes/Point:: in the view's own coordinates.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String::.

METHOD:: busLabel
What the drop-down of a bus module says: the BusManager name it reads or writes to - with a
code::?:: after it while BusManager knows no bus of that name - code::bus 16  ar 2::
for an index of your own, with its rate and width; code::pick a bus:: before it
has one.

METHOD:: speakersLabel
What the drop-down of a panner - code::vbap::, code::ambi:: - says, in the place
of a bus module's: its speaker setup's label and channels, code::octo   8::, or
the name with a code::?:: while link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers:: has no such
setup. Click it for the setups.

METHOD:: speakersText
What the hint bar says of it: the setup, its speakers and its sub.

METHOD:: busText
What the hint bar says of it, over the drop-down and the title: whether it
reads the bus, writes to it or both, which bus, its index, rate and channels -
or why there is none.

METHOD:: front
Bring the view up over the other modules - what a drag does first.
